              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.094,
            0.282,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Mean Foot"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.11,
            0.557,
            0.319
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the early 1500s in Europe, the basic unit of land measurement was the rod, defined as 16 feet long. At that time, a foot meant the length of a human foot! But foot sizes vary, so whose foot could they measure? To solve this, 16 adult males were asked to stand in a line, toe to heel, and the length of that line was considered the 16-foot rod. After the rod was determined, it was split into 16 equal sections, each representing the measure of a single foot. In essence, this was the arithmetic mean of the 16 individual feet, even though the term ‘mean’ was not mentioned anywhere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.568,
